        "## Mathematical Framework: World Cup Modeling & Feature Engineering\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1. Limitations of the Basic Poisson Model\n",
        "\n",
        "The standard baseline models football scores as two independent Poisson distributions for the home and away goals:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$X \\sim \\text{Poisson}(\\lambda), \\quad Y \\sim \\text{Poisson}(\\mu)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Where the probability of a specific scoreline $(x, y)$ is given by:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$P(X = x, Y = y) = P(X = x) \\cdot P(Y = y) = \\frac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!} \\cdot \\frac{\\mu^y e^{-\\mu}}{y!}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "While this provides a tractable approximation, it suffers from two major domain-specific failures:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Independence Assumption Failure:** High-scoring outcomes may display independence, but low-scoring outcomes ($0\\text{--}0, 1\\text{--}0, 0\\text{--}1, 1\\text{--}1$) are correlated. Teams alter tactics based on the live score state (e.g., playing defensively after scoring first).\n",
        "2. **Underestimation of Low-Score Draws:** Empirically, professional football produces significantly more $0\\text{--}0$ and $1\\text{--}1$ draws than an independent double-Poisson distribution predicts.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## 2. The Dixon-Coles Model\n",
        "\n",
        "The Dixon-Coles (1997) model addresses these deficiencies by introducing two critical modifications: a time-decay weighting function and an outcome-dependence parameter ($\\rho$).\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2.1 Time-Decay Weighting\n",
        "\n",
        "Older matches are less representative of a team's current ability. Match $i$ at historical date $t_i$ relative to the prediction cutoff date $T$ is assigned an exponential weight $w_i$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$w_i = 0.5^{\\frac{T - t_i}{\\xi}}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Where $\\xi$ is the half-life parameter (in days). A match played $\\xi$ days prior receives half the weight of a match played today.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2.2 Model Parameterization\n",
        "\n",
        "For team $i$ playing against team $j$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\lambda_{ij} = \\exp(\\alpha_i - \\beta_j + \\gamma \\cdot h)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\mu_{ij} = \\exp(\\alpha_j - \\beta_i)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Where:\n",
        "* $\\alpha_i \\in \\mathbb{R}$: Attack strength of team $i$\n",
        "* $\\beta_i \\in \\mathbb{R}$: Defense strength of team $i$\n",
        "* $\\gamma \\in \\mathbb{R}$: Home field advantage multiplier\n",
        "* $h \\in \\{0, 1\\}$: Indicator variable ($1$ if non-neutral venue, $0$ otherwise)\n",
        "\n",
        "To ensure model identifiability, a sum-to-zero constraint is applied to the attack parameters:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\sum_{k=1}^{N} \\alpha_k = 0$$\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2.3 The Tau ($\\tau$) Dependence Factor\n",
        "\n",
        "To adjust for low-score correlation, Dixon-Coles introduces an adjustment factor $\\tau_{\\lambda, \\mu}(x, y)$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\tau_{\\lambda, \\mu}(x, y) = \\begin{cases}\n",
        "1 - \\lambda \\mu \\rho & \\text{if } x = 0, y = 0 \\\\\n",
        "1 + \\lambda \\rho & \\text{if } x = 0, y = 1 \\\\\n",
        "1 + \\mu \\rho & \\text{if } x = 1, y = 0 \\\\\n",
        "1 - \\rho & \\text{if } x = 1, y = 1 \\\\\n",
        "1 & \\text{if } x + y > 2\n",
        "\\end{cases}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Where $\\rho$ is the dependency parameter between the two teams' scores.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2.4 Likelihood Optimization\n",
        "\n",
        "The joint probability density function for match $k$ with scores $(x_k, y_k)$ is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$P(X_k = x_k, Y_k = y_k \\mid \\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\rho) = \\tau_{\\lambda_k, \\mu_k}(x_k, y_k) \\cdot \\left( \\frac{\\lambda_k^{x_k} e^{-\\lambda_k}}{x_k!} \\right) \\cdot \\left( \\frac{\\mu_k^{y_k} e^{-\\mu_k}}{y_k!} \\right)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The parameters $\\mathbf{\\theta} = (\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}, \\boldsymbol{\\beta}, \\gamma, \\rho)$ are estimated by minimizing the weighted negative log-likelihood:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbf{\\theta}) = -\\sum_{k=1}^{M} w_k \\cdot \\left[ \\ln\\left(\\tau_{\\lambda_k, \\mu_k}(x_k, y_k)\\right) + x_k \\ln(\\lambda_k) - \\lambda_k - \\ln(x_k!) + y_k \\ln(\\mu_k) - \\mu_k - \\ln(y_k!) \\right]$$\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## 3. Dixon-Coles Features for XGBoost Integration\n",
        "\n",
        "The parameters estimated by Dixon-Coles are leveraged to generate structured features for gradient boosted decision trees.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a match between home team $h$ and away team $a$ at date $t$:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Expected Home Goals ($\\lambda_{dc}$):**\n",
        "   $$\\lambda_{dc} = \\exp(\\hat{\\alpha}_h - \\hat{\\beta}_a + \\hat{\\gamma} \\cdot h)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "2. **Expected Away Goals ($\\mu_{dc}$):**\n",
        "   $$\\mu_{dc} = \\exp(\\hat{\\alpha}_a - \\hat{\\beta}_h)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "3. **Attack Differential ($\\text{att\\_diff}$):**\n",
        "   $$\\text{att\\_diff} = \\hat{\\alpha}_h - \\hat{\\alpha}_a$$\n",
        "\n",
        "These parameters condense long-term historical efficiency into low-dimensional representations, which are then combined with short-term form metrics (rolling 5-match points, goals scored, and goals conceded) to form the feature matrix $X$.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## 4. Bayesian Hierarchical Modeling via MCMC\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4.1 Concept\n",
        "\n",
        "Point-estimation methods (like Maximum Likelihood in Dixon-Coles) yield single estimates for parameters. Bayesian inference models parameters as probability distributions, capturing uncertainty due to limited international sample sizes.\n",
        "\n",
        "Given observed match data $\\mathcal{D}$, we estimate the joint posterior distribution of the parameters $P(\\mathbf{\\theta} \\mid \\mathcal{D})$ using Bayes' Theorem:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$P(\\mathbf{\\theta} \\mid \\mathcal{D}) = \\frac{P(\\mathcal{D} \\mid \\mathbf{\\theta}) P(\\mathbf{\\theta})}{P(\\mathcal{D})} \\propto P(\\mathcal{D} \\mid \\mathbf{\\theta}) P(\\mathbf{\\theta})$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Because the marginal likelihood $P(\\mathcal{D}) = \\int P(\\mathcal{D} \\mid \\mathbf{\\theta}) P(\\mathbf{\\theta}) d\\mathbf{\\theta}$ is analytically intractable for non-trivial models, we draw samples from the posterior distribution using Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC).\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4.2 Non-Centered Parameterization\n",
        "\n",
        "To avoid geometry issues like Neal's Funnel during sampling, attack and defense parameters are specified using a non-centered parameterization:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\sigma_{att} \\sim \\text{HalfNormal}(1.0), \\quad \\sigma_{def} \\sim \\text{HalfNormal}(1.0)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\mathbf{\\alpha}_{\\text{raw}} \\sim \\text{Normal}(0, \\mathbf{I}), \\quad \\mathbf{\\beta}_{\\text{raw}} \\sim \\text{Normal}(0, \\mathbf{I})$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\mathbf{\\alpha} = \\mathbf{\\alpha}_{\\text{raw}} \\cdot \\sigma_{att}, \\quad \\mathbf{\\beta} = \\mathbf{\\beta}_{\\text{raw}} \\cdot \\sigma_{def}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This separation of parameter scale ($\\sigma$) from latent location ($\\mathbf{\\alpha}_{\\text{raw}}$) stabilizes Hamiltonian Monte Carlo step sizing and speeds up convergence.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4.3 MCMC Engine: No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS)\n",
        "\n",
        "The implementation utilizes NUTS (an adaptive extension of Hamiltonian Monte Carlo). NUTS simulates Hamiltonian dynamics over the continuous posterior surface, generating proposal steps using gradients calculated via automatic differentiation. This allows efficient exploration of high-dimensional parameter spaces without random walk behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## 5. XGBoost Regressor with Poisson Objective\n",
        "\n",
        "The final layer models expected goal parameters ($\\lambda_{xgb}, \\mu_{xgb}$) non-parametrically using gradient boosted decision trees.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5.1 Poisson Loss Function\n",
        "\n",
        "Standard Mean Squared Error assumes Gaussian errors, which is inappropriate for non-negative count data. Instead, XGBoost optimizes the Poisson objective:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{Poisson}}(y, \\hat{y}) = \\sum_{i=1}^N \\left( \\hat{y}_i - y_i \\ln(\\hat{y}_i) \\right)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Where $\\hat{y}_i = \\exp(f(x_i))$, guaranteeing strictly positive expected values $\\lambda > 0$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5.2 Score Probability Matrix Generation\n",
        "\n",
        "For any trained model yielding expected goals $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$, the complete score probability matrix $M \\in \\mathbb{R}^{G \\times G}$ (up to max goals $G-1$) is computed via the outer product of their marginal distributions:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$M_{i,j} = P(X = i) \\cdot P(Y = j) = \\left( \\frac{\\lambda^i e^{-\\lambda}}{i!} \\right) \\cdot \\left( \\frac{\\mu^j e^{-\\mu}}{j!} \\right)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For Dixon-Coles, $M_{i,j}$ is further adjusted by $\\tau_{\\lambda, \\mu}(i, j)$ and normalized such that $\\sum_{i,j} M_{i,j} = 1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5.3 Outcome Aggregation (1X2 Probabilities)\n",
        "\n",
        "From matrix $M$, match outcome probabilities are derived:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\text{P(Home Win)} = \\sum_{i > j} M_{i,j}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\text{P(Draw)} = \\sum_{i = j} M_{i,j} = \\text{trace}(M)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\text{P(Away Win)} = \\sum_{i < j} M_{i,j}$$\n"
